How the content is made, and what that means for this license
-------------------------------------------------------------

Most pages are written by language models from published research,
under rules, templates and checks that people designed, and
maintained by the Learning Design Alliance. The project's structure,
schemas, templates, editorial rules, selection of sources and code
are human work.

In several jurisdictions, including the United States, text produced
by an AI system without enough human creative control is not protected
by copyright. Some of these pages may therefore carry no copyright,
and to that extent they are in the public domain. This license applies
to the extent that copyright or similar rights exist in the content;
it claims nothing beyond them. Either way, you may do at least what
the license allows, and nobody is restricted by it.

Pages quote short passages from the research they summarise, with a
citation. Those passages remain the work of their authors and are
reproduced as quotation; this license does not cover them.

This note describes the project's position. It is not legal advice.

Software in this repository (everything under scripts/, and any other
source code) is licensed separately under the MIT License — see
LICENSE-CODE.
